Below is a **complete, self‑contained Java program** that demonstrates the most common ways to use generics:

* A generic **`Box<T>`** class that can hold any type.
* A **bounded generic class** `NumberBox<T extends Number>` that only accepts numeric types.
* A **generic method** `swap` that works on any `List<E>`.
* **Wildcard usage** (`? extends`, `? super`) with the standard library `List`.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

/**
 * 1️⃣  Generic class – can store *any* type
 */
class Box<T> {
    private T value;              // the contained value

    public Box(T value) {
        this.value = value;
    }

    public T get() {
        return value;
    }

    public void set(T value) {
        this.value = value;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Box{" + "value=" + value + '}';
    }
}

/**
 * 2️⃣  Bounded generic – only numeric types allowed
 */
class NumberBox<T extends Number> {
    private T number;

    public NumberBox(T number) {
        this.number = number;
    }

    public double getDoubleValue() {
        // All Numbers expose doubleValue()
        return number.doubleValue();
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "NumberBox{" + "number=" + number + '}';
    }
}

/**
 * 3️⃣  Generic method – works for any list element type
 */
class Util {
    // Swaps the first two elements in any List<E>
    public static <E> void swap(List<E> list) {
        if (list.size() < 2) return; // nothing to swap
        E tmp = list.get(0);
        list.set(0, list.get(1));
        list.set(1, tmp);
    }
}

public class GenericsDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        /* ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
           1️⃣  Generic Box – holds a String
        ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
        Box<String> stringBox = new Box<>("Hello, Generics!");
        System.out.println(stringBox); // Box{value=Hello, Generics!}

        /* ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
           1️⃣  Generic Box – holds an Integer
        ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
        Box<Integer> intBox = new Box<>(42);
        System.out.println(intBox); // Box{value=42}

        /* ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
           2️⃣  NumberBox – only accepts numeric types
        ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
        NumberBox<Double> doubleBox = new NumberBox<>(3.1415);
        System.out.println(doubleBox);           // NumberBox{number=3.1415}
        System.out.println("doubleValue = " + doubleBox.getDoubleValue()); // 3.1415

        // The following would not compile:
        // NumberBox<String> invalid = new NumberBox<>("oops"); // ❌ error

        /* ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
           3️⃣  Generic method swap – works for List<Integer> and List<String>
        ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
        List<Integer> ints = new ArrayList<>();
        ints.add(1);
        ints.add(2);
        ints.add(3);
        System.out.println("Before swap: " + ints);
        Util.swap(ints);
        System.out.println("After swap: " + ints);

        List<String> strs = new ArrayList<>();
        strs.add("first");
        strs.add("second");
        System.out.println("\nBefore swap: " + strs);
        Util.swap(strs);
        System.out.println("After swap: " + strs);

        /* ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
           4️⃣  Wildcards – ? extends Number (read‑only), ? super Integer (write‑only)
        ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
        List<? extends Number> numberList = List.of(1, 2.5, 3L);
        // We can read values, but cannot add anything (compiler error):
        Number n = numberList.get(0); // OK
        // numberList.add(4);          // ❌ compile error

        List<? super Integer> superIntList = new ArrayList<>();
        superIntList.add(10);          // OK – we can add an Integer
        // Integer i = superIntList.get(0); // ❌ compile error – type unknown

        // Note: To iterate safely, use a wildcard capture
        printNumbers(numberList);
    }

    // A helper method that uses wildcard capture
    private static void printNumbers(List<? extends Number> list) {
        for (Number num : list) {
            System.out.println("Number: " + num);
        }
    }
}
```

### What the code shows

| Feature | Code snippet | Why it matters |
|---------|--------------|----------------|
| **Generic class** `Box<T>` | `Box<String> stringBox = new Box<>("text");` | Stores *any* type safely without casting. |
| **Bounded generic** `NumberBox<T extends Number>` | `NumberBox<Double> doubleBox = new NumberBox<>(3.14);` | Restricts the type parameter to a known hierarchy. |
| **Generic method** `swap` | `Util.swap(list);` | A method that works for *any* list element type. |
| **Wildcards** (`? extends`, `? super`) | `List<? extends Number> numberList = List.of(1, 2.5);` | Provides flexibility while preserving type safety. |

Compile and run the program to see the output illustrating each concept. This concise example captures the core of generics in Java and is ready for inclusion in a learning or interview setting.
